{
  "sourceId": "post-Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator-en",
  "locale": "en",
  "source": {
    "kind": "single-post",
    "postId": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator"
  },
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "↵For a long time, the most frequent complaints about the Android emulator were its slow startup and operational speeds.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Using the Intel Atom x86 system image helped slightly, but not significantly.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "However, last year, Intel released the Intel Hardware Accelerated Execution Manager (HAXM), which dramatically improved the situation.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Previously, it took several minutes from the Android boot animation to reach the screen lock, but now it takes just 10 seconds!",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "First, ensure your CPU supports VT, which most CPUs today do.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Then, go to the Extras section in the Android SDK Manager and install the Intel x86 Emulator Accelerator (HAXM).",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Note that this step only downloads the package; you must complete the installation by locating it in the SDK Path.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "For example, on a Mac, it is downloaded to SDK Path /extras/intel/Hardware_Accelerated_Execution_Manager.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "You then open the IntelHAXM.dmg file and run the package inside to finish the installation.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "After running the Emulator, if successful, you will see in the Console: Emulator] HAX is working and emulator runs in fast virt mode.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "If you want to see how fast the Android emulator runs on a MacBook Air, you can watch the following video.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "type": "post",
        "id": "Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator",
        "title": "Accelerate the Android Emulator",
        "url": "/blog/Accelerate-the-Android-Emulator/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "{% youtube 1jwX_n8B3mI %}↵",
